==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*flektō||}}, of {{unc|la|nocap=1}} ultimate origin, lacking any solid Indo-European cognates. Possible {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} predecessors include {{m|ine-pro|*bʰleK-||}}, {{m|ine-pro|*dʰleK-||}}, and {{m|ine-pro|*gʷʰleK-||}}.

Matasović has connected {{m|la|flectō}} with {{cog|sla-pro|*gleznъ|t=ankle}}, reconstructing {{der|la|ine-pro|*gʷʰleǵʰ-}} to account for both.<ref name="Matasović 2017">{{cite-journal| last=Matasović | first=Ranko | title=Latin presents in -t- and the etymologies of ''necto'' ‘to weave, bind’ and ''flecto'' ‘to bend, curve’ | journal=Pallas | publisher=Presses Universitaires du Midi | issue=103 | year=2017 | issn=2272-7639 | jstor=26576272 | pages=37–43 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/26576272 | access-date=June 5, 2023}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|flectō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|flectō|flex|flex}}

# to [[bend]], [[curve]] or [[bow]]
# to [[deviate]], [[distract]]
#: {{syn|la|arceō|prōpulsō|dīvertō|dēclīnō|āvertō|āspernor|dēmoveō|dēflectō|trānsvertō}}
# to [[turn]] or [[curl]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|155|thru=156|quote=[...] genitor caelōque invectus apertō<br/>'''flectit''' equōs currūque volāns dat lōra secundō.|trans=''[...] Father [Neptune], [now] riding under a clear sky, '''turns''' [his] team and gives rein to [his] swift-following chariot [as it] flies.''}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} to [[persuade]], [[prevail]] upon, or [[soften]]
#: {{syn|la|persuādeō|convincō|trahō|perpellō|admoneō}}

